  "identity": {
    "playing_style": "Simeone's Atlético remain a defence-first team that shifts between a 4-4-2 and a 5-3-2 depending on the opponent, defending compactly and attacking through quick transitions and set pieces. 2025/26 was more open than the Cholo norm (62 scored, 44 conceded in La Liga) and the team was far better in knockout football (UCL semi-final, Copa final) than over 38 league rounds (4th, 69 points, 25 behind Barcelona). The 2026 window added technical quality (Lee Kang-in, Grimaldo, Hjulmand) and a ball-playing captain-type centre-back (Romero) after Griezmann, Molina and Almada left.",
    "principles": [
      "coaching continuity: Simeone in charge since December 2011, longest tenure in Europe's top five leagues",
      "defensive organisation and intensity as identity ('Cholismo')",
      "sustained investment funded by large sales and MLS/South American exits (Griezmann, Almada, Ruggeri, Molina) plus Apollo capital",
      "Argentine core (Simeone, Romero, Álvarez, Giuliano Simeone, Musso)",
      "mid-season opportunism (Lookman, Mendoza, Vargas signed in February 2026)"
    ],
    "club_character": "Second club of Madrid with a fiercely loyal fanbase and a self-image as the underdog against Real Madrid; 70k Riyadh Air Metropolitano; high-pressure Madrid media environment but unusual stability under a 14-year coaching reign. Ownership is private (Gil Marín / Cerezo holding with Quantum Pacific, plus Apollo Sports Capital investment announced in 2025) with an ambitious Ciudad del Deporte real-estate project around the stadium.",
    "ownership": "private (Atlético HoldCo: Miguel Ángel Gil Marín, Enrique Cerezo, Quantum Pacific/Idan Ofer; Apollo Sports Capital investment agreed 2025)",
    "political_and_social_context": "Publicly documented: the 2025 sale of a majority stake to Apollo Sports Capital and the Riyadh Air stadium naming deal drew media scrutiny over foreign/state-linked capital; the club has faced repeated UEFA/La Liga sanctions and partial stadium closures for racist or discriminatory chanting by sections of supporters (e.g. 2024 Champions League and Madrid derby incidents). No further institution-level positions verified this session.",
    "languages": [
      "es",
      "en",
      "pt"
    ],
    "wage_structure": "hierarchical (highest earners historically Griezmann, Oblak, Álvarez; Griezmann's exit in 2026 freed the largest salary) — details unverified",
    "transfer_policy": "Prime-age international starters bought for EUR 20-40m (Lookman, Romero, Hjulmand, Lee, Grimaldo) and funded by selling recent signings at close to cost (Ruggeri ~EUR 20m, Molina ~EUR 18m, Almada EUR 10m); heavy use of loans with buy options (Jonathan David, Nico González in 2025/26); Argentine/South American pipeline.",
    "injury_management_reputation": null
  },
